---
import StateChip from '../../components/StateChip.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const facts = [
  ['started', 'first commit in August 2025; in production today'],
  ['stack', 'PHP 8.5 · Laravel 13 · Filament 5 · Livewire 4 · Octane on FrankenPHP · PostgreSQL'],
  ['tests', '~290 Pest test files, feature and unit, plus architecture rules'],
  ['team', 'built and run solo; sales by a partner'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Teuka case study · Nelson Otazo"
  description="How I built and run Teuka solo: a multi-tenant Laravel SaaS for scientific conferences, with hand-rolled tenancy, ~290 test files and automated deploys."
  lang="en"
>
  <article class="case">
    <header class="case__head">
      <p class="case__path mono"><a href="/#work">← /work</a></p>
      <div class="case__title">
        <h1 class="mono">teuka</h1>
        <StateChip state="production" />
      </div>
      <p class="case__lead">
        A multi-tenant SaaS for scientific conferences: abstract submission, double-blind peer review, registrations,
        payments and certificates. I built it and run it solo, from the database schema to the deploy pipeline.
      </p>
      <p class="case__links mono">
        <a href="https://teuka.app">teuka.app</a> · closed source
      </p>
    </header>

    <dl class="card facts mono">
      {
        facts.map(([term, detail]) => (
          <div class="facts__row">
            <dt>{term}</dt>
            <dd>{detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <section aria-labelledby="architecture">
      <h2 id="architecture" class="section-label">// architecture</h2>
      <p>
        A modular monolith. Each business area is a module with its own models, migrations, policies and tests:
        abstracts, evaluations, conferences, registrations, payments, certificates, users and organizations. Four
        Filament panels sit on top, one per kind of user: platform admin, organization admin, conference participants
        and reviewers.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Payment gateways plug in behind one interface, so adding a gateway does not touch registrations. A local
          online-payment gateway runs in production.
        </li>
        <li>Certificates are rendered to PDF on request and sent as signed links, so no generated file is stored.</li>
        <li>
          Pest architecture rules keep the boundaries honest, for example "payment partners never reach the
          presentation layer" and "no Eloquent model lives outside a Models namespace".
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="tenancy">
      <h2 id="tenancy" class="section-label">// multi-tenancy</h2>
      <p>
        Hand-rolled, without a tenancy package: one PostgreSQL database where every tenant-owned row carries its
        organization, and every isolation rule lives in application code covered by tests.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The organization is resolved from an exact match on the request host. The match is deliberately not
          normalized: a looser match could hand one organization's pages to another.
        </li>
        <li>
          Eloquent global scopes filter every query by organization, and role scopes narrow it further: participants
          see their own submissions, reviewers only the abstracts assigned to them.
        </li>
        <li>Policies check the organization again, and panels answer 404 when no organization resolves.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="review">
      <h2 id="review" class="section-label">// double-blind review</h2>
      <p>
        Reviewers never see who wrote an abstract, and authors never see who reviewed it. Only the organizing committee
        sees both. This is enforced by policies and by what each panel renders, not by convention.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="testing">
      <h2 id="testing" class="section-label">// testing</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>~290 Pest test files: feature tests through the panels and HTTP layer, unit tests for the domain.</li>
        <li>Guards for the failures that hurt in production: migration rollbacks, route caching and N+1 queries.</li>
        <li>Every pull request runs the suite in parallel against PostgreSQL, plus Pint for code style.</li>
        <li>Larastan (level 5) runs locally before changes go up.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I work with AI coding agents every day. The test suite and the architecture rules are what make that fast
        without making it reckless.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="delivery">
      <h2 id="delivery" class="section-label">// delivery</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Every merge to main builds a Docker image on FrankenPHP, pushes it to the GitHub container registry and
          deploys it through Coolify.
        </li>
        <li>Supervisor runs Octane, the queue worker and the scheduler inside the container.</li>
        <li>Sentry reports errors and Laravel Pulse tracks performance in production.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .case {
    padding-block: var(--space-7);
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .case__head {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .case__path {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .case__title {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .case__title h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 2.5rem);
  }

  .case__lead {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .case__links {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .facts {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-7);
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .facts__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 6rem 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .facts dt {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .facts dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-7);
  }

  section p,
  section li {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-ink-body);
  }

  section p + ul,
  section ul + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  section ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  section li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .facts__row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
